\f4\fs26 The mammoth size of the proposed funeral ceremony caused the cabinet a great deal of concern. Every available Elvi would be present, forcing them to commandeer the decrepit old sports stadium in the quarter, long abandoned and overgrown. According to legend, long ago this tired structure had hosted a big international sports carnival involving, some said, Greeks running around nude, but it had been rarely used since. The exterior of the concrete and steel monolith, sprawling across several hectares of mud and slop, was completely covered in slippery moss, fungus and lichen with sickly weeds poking out of every crack and join.\
Since the moment of Kleo\'92s passing, one in ten Elvi had spent every waking moment clearing, cleaning and buffing the entire stadium. Some of the grandstands were well past their use-by date and had the be condemned, but that did not mean they could not be filled to the brim with flashing lights and speakers. Due to lack of grass growing anywhere in the PTD, a lawn mower had not been manufactured for about 50 years, but would have come in handy. The field was mowed perfectly flat to what resembled a lawn, but was really a collection of weeds and undergrowth that had been hacked down to the same length by 1,000 Elvis wielding 1,000 pair of clippers. At full capacity the ground held 80,000 or so in the stadium, but with the a few of the stands closed, the Elvi were planning to squeeze in 65,000, with the rest of their number forming the funeral parade.\
\
Hayley arrived in the stadium just in time for the official ceremony to begin. Seated in the private box at the southern end of the ground was the official entourage of the High Elvis, the Holy Mother and the Great Teacher. In the centre of the box was Sarah who, it appeared to Hayley, was the subject of reverence and love by every Elvis and Elvine. They adored her and also, much to her amazement, they seemed to have high regard for Cracker. 
\f5\i A level 2 nobody is The Great Teacher? How ludicrous.
\f4\i0  It seemed, for whatever reason, they occupied an influential position in the quarter. As Hayley sat down she was pleased to see Dr Logan, seated in between Cracker and the High Elvis, was on her best behaviour, not giggling or fidgeting - not one bit. Her amazement morphed to amusement when she gleaned, from the conversations in the private box, that the these black haired entertainment clones had formed themselves a little government and were passing laws. Although the ideas of an ethnic minority creating their own parliament tickled her funny bone, but she knew that the Moral Compliance Directorate would take a very dim view. This was a challenge to the legal authority of the Primary Trade Dominion and the novice government will be soon crushed under foot.\
\
The lights dimmed throughout the stadium and kettle drums beat out a tribal rhythm as the first of approximately 25,000 fire-torch wielding Elvi entered the stadium at the northern end. In the darkness the torches were thrown high into the air and between the participants in a choreographed, well practiced but dangerous dance of flames. The kettle drums became louder and more insistent as the flames continued to dance about in a manically, before the Elvi commenced stomping, clapping and singing a foot-tapping funeral song. The drums continued but faded into the background of resurgent stomping and clapping in syncopation. \
